WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.264 --> 00:00:03.400
By the end of this video you'll know
how to create a simple database schema.

2
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.594
Using SQL you'll do this
by building the schema for

3
00:00:06.594 --> 00:00:10.020
a shopping cart database
consisting of three tables.

4
00:00:10.020 --> 00:00:14.397
Let's start by creating a new
database called shopping cart TB.

5
00:00:14.397 --> 00:00:18.449
First I type the create

6
00:00:18.449 --> 00:00:23.782
database keyword followed by

7
00:00:23.782 --> 00:00:28.694
the name shopping cart TB.

8
00:00:28.694 --> 00:00:34.731
Then I run the statement,
the shopping cart DB.

9
00:00:34.731 --> 00:00:38.070
Database appears in
the left hand Explorer.

10
00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:45.228
Now it can create the tables
inside this database.

11
00:00:45.228 --> 00:00:50.013
First I need to create the customer table
which stores the following information on

12
00:00:50.013 --> 00:00:51.663
each customer customer ID.

13
00:00:51.663 --> 00:00:57.169
Name, address, email and
phone number to create this table.

14
00:00:57.169 --> 00:01:00.963
I used to create table keyword and

15
00:01:00.963 --> 00:01:06.115
then I type customer
followed by parentheses

16
00:01:06.115 --> 00:01:10.993
in the parentheses I
specify the fields and

17
00:01:10.993 --> 00:01:14.397
their data types as follows.

18
00:01:14.397 --> 00:01:15.280
The customer ID.

19
00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.093
Data type is integer while
the others are bar char.

20
00:01:19.093 --> 00:01:23.823
I give the name and
email fields a character limit of 100.

21
00:01:23.823 --> 00:01:27.908
I assign a character limit of 255 for
address And

22
00:01:27.908 --> 00:01:31.812
I assign the limit of 10 characters for
phone.

23
00:01:31.812 --> 00:01:35.887
Also note that I've used the primary
key keyword on the customer ID.

24
00:01:35.887 --> 00:01:36.679
Column.

25
00:01:36.679 --> 00:01:39.810
This designates that field as
the primary key of the table.

26
00:01:39.810 --> 00:01:42.693
A role you'll learn more about soon.

27
00:01:42.693 --> 00:01:46.429
Next is the product table
which stores the product ID.

28
00:01:46.429 --> 00:01:49.902
Name, price and description.

29
00:01:49.902 --> 00:01:58.265
I can specify this table as follows.

30
00:01:58.265 --> 00:01:59.267
The product ID.

31
00:01:59.267 --> 00:02:01.164
has the integer data type.

32
00:02:01.164 --> 00:02:04.461
The name is of our char
with a 100 character limit.

33
00:02:04.461 --> 00:02:08.877
The price has a numeric type
with parameters of eight and 2.

34
00:02:08.877 --> 00:02:13.327
The description is var char
with a 255 character limit.

35
00:02:13.327 --> 00:02:14.385
And the product ID.

36
00:02:14.385 --> 00:02:17.427
Is set as the primary
key within this table.

37
00:02:17.427 --> 00:02:21.952
And finally there's the cart order
table which holds the order ID.

38
00:02:21.952 --> 00:02:23.216
Customer ID.

39
00:02:23.216 --> 00:02:24.446
Product ID.

40
00:02:24.446 --> 00:02:28.341
Quantity, order, date and status.

41
00:02:28.341 --> 00:02:33.316
This table is set up as follows

42
00:02:33.316 --> 00:02:38.097
the order ID, customer ID.

43
00:02:38.097 --> 00:02:41.463
Product ID and
quantity are all integer types.

44
00:02:41.463 --> 00:02:47.569
Order, date is date and status is
var char with a 100 character limit.

45
00:02:47.569 --> 00:02:48.298
Order ID.

46
00:02:48.298 --> 00:02:49.807
Is the primary key here.

47
00:02:49.807 --> 00:02:54.135
However, this table also introduces
something new in the form of two

48
00:02:54.135 --> 00:02:55.112
foreign keys.

49
00:02:55.112 --> 00:03:00.674
Before moving forward, let's quickly
discuss what primary and foreign keys are.

50
00:03:00.674 --> 00:03:04.204
You may have noticed that the cart
order table contains the customer ID.

51
00:03:04.204 --> 00:03:06.692
And product ID fields.

52
00:03:06.692 --> 00:03:10.851
These same fields are also
found in the other two tables.

53
00:03:10.851 --> 00:03:15.471
This is because these fields in the cart
order table are directly linked

54
00:03:15.471 --> 00:03:18.637
to the same fields in
the corresponding tables.

55
00:03:18.637 --> 00:03:23.529
To establish this relationship,
each table must contain a primary key.

56
00:03:23.529 --> 00:03:27.672
The referencing table then uses
foreign keys that pointed to

57
00:03:27.672 --> 00:03:31.255
the external source table or
the referenced table.

58
00:03:31.255 --> 00:03:36.614
You learn more about primary and foreign
keys in greater detail in a later lesson.

59
00:03:36.614 --> 00:03:41.019
But for now let's return to
the shopping cart database example.

60
00:03:41.019 --> 00:03:43.431
All primary keys have been set up.

61
00:03:43.431 --> 00:03:45.784
So the foreign keys for
the card order table.

62
00:03:45.784 --> 00:03:50.766
Come next I create the first one
by using the foreign key key word,

63
00:03:50.766 --> 00:03:52.947
along with the customer ID.

64
00:03:52.947 --> 00:03:56.354
Column name to link it to
the customer ID field.

65
00:03:56.354 --> 00:04:01.059
In the customer table, I then used
a references keyword followed by

66
00:04:01.059 --> 00:04:04.951
the source table name,
customer and then customer ID.

67
00:04:04.951 --> 00:04:11.007
And parenthesis creating a foreign key for
product ideas similar but

68
00:04:11.007 --> 00:04:14.987
with product and product ideas references.

69
00:04:14.987 --> 00:04:20.307
So I used the foreign key keyword and
name it product ID.

70
00:04:20.307 --> 00:04:23.286
I then reference it in
the product source table.

71
00:04:23.286 --> 00:04:27.937
Product and then product ID.

72
00:04:27.937 --> 00:04:31.662
Then I execute these statements and

73
00:04:31.662 --> 00:04:36.636
the tables appear nested
beneath shopping ID.

74
00:04:36.636 --> 00:04:38.898
In the left hand Explorer.

75
00:04:38.898 --> 00:04:41.042
In this video you learned the steps for

76
00:04:41.042 --> 00:04:43.664
creating a simple database
schema using SQL.

77
00:04:43.664 --> 00:04:47.571
The same process applies for
both small and large scale databases.